on-arrival: {! Q.cyprus_campaign_report=AnatolianRules.cyprusCampaign.report(Q,23); Q.cyprus_atilla1_briefing = AnatolianRules.cyprusAtilla1.briefing(Q, 23); !}


= July 23, 1974: A Ceasefire on Paper Only

Our continuing military advances have seen the end of Greek Junta with Karamanlis's arrival, followed immediately by Sampson's overthrow and his replacement by Clerides. These events were met with mixed feelings in Turkey, for in every Turk's mind, Karamanlis was known as a man who had signed the London-Zurich agreements despite Makarios and believed in Turkish-Greek friendship... Clerides too had always inspired sympathy with his moderate and reasonable attitude. The accession of these 2 figures had increased the hopes for a way of solution for Cyprus to open. In the circles of our government, the voices of "Having had brought democracy to Greece" was loud, a softer atmosphere had emerged with the arrival of Karamanlis as the possibility of a new and wide-ranging cooperation could be established between Turkey and Greece...

However, these hopes and prayers were met with dissatisfcation as the Greek side's ferment and absolute, unmoveable stance on refusal of any prospect of terriotarial autonomy for Turks in Cyprus, which was the center point of our Denktaş Plan, which had sought to create a Turkish zone in the North of Cyprus that'd make up 34% of the island as a federal state inside an united Cypriote republic.

And in Cyprus, the ceasefire existed only in words. Turkey was continually massing troops to make up for the diplomatic shortfall and trying to establish itself fully on the territory it held and ensure its security further. Small skirmishes continued in the Beşparmak Mountains and the complete takeover of Kyrenia was still not achieved. With the ceasefire appearing even effective than any expectations, the Turkish army kept advancing as it's Airborne forces had entered the districts of Nicosia since yesterday and were now moving Westward toward the Airport.

Our military advancement since yesterday had a varying degree of success, and we once again possessed the possibility of continuing to advance while maintaining diplomatic talks, or we can finally adhere by an increasingly weaker ceasefire to show good faith, however we would be entrusting the UN, an organization that was incapable of enforcing it ceasefire, for giving protection to exposed Turkish communities across Cyprus from frenzied Cypriot Militias....

- @historical: Continue consolidation while entering diplomatic talks
- @alternative: Halt offensive movement and seek UN protection for exposed communities

